{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:rstar:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:0461bb808d4bb5903096ce85507b0588c30ff606777ba975f877ddadb83f7419","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.85},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.85},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.75}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR ADOPTED: Historian, P10 1.0 / P50 2.05 / P90 2.90, built from a mechanical centre of 1.58% (0.60 x 0.90 OECD + 0.40 x 2.60 China) plus 0.4-0.5pp of healthy-aging and productivity offset. STRUCTURAL CONSTRAINT FROM FERMI (not an update, a check on the prior's internals): the Historian's China leg of 2.60% is inconsistent with the locked -2.7pp drag. Fermi built China twice — bottom-up (-0.40 labour + 1.60 capital + 0.70 TFP - 0.30 frictions = 1.60%) and top-down (-2.7pp against a ~4.3% no-aging convergence trend = 1.60%) — and the two methods agree to the decimal. Allowing his stated reason for moving up to 2.00% (the -2.7pp is a 2025-2050 average and the dependency shift is steeper after 2035, so the early-2030s drag is nearer -2.2pp), China lands at 1.8-2.1%, not 2.6%. Re-running the Historian's own blend at the agreed 2031 PPP weights (0.63/0.37, which Fermi and the sim adapter independently reconcile at 0.629/0.371 — an accounting fact I adopt, unlike the model's growth rates) with China at 2.0% and the OECD at 1.1-1.2% gives 1.43-1.50%, plus the offsets, so the Historian's 2.05 rests on a China leg his own effort's structure does not support. EVIDENCE AND LRs. E1, synchronised multi-decade highs at the global long end: DFII10 2.60% on 30 Oct 2026 and 2.62% on 2 Nov, DFII30 3.06-3.08%, JGB 30y all-time high 4.235% intraday 5 Oct 2026, UK 30y gilt reportedly ~5.75% at 1998 highs (UNVERIFIED AT THE SCOUT'S HORIZON, zero weight). Raw LR for the low-growth branch 2.0:1, but the signal is genuinely AMBIGUOUS — a high real rate is equally the signature of a high marginal product of capital, which is growth-POSITIVE. The disambiguation is the shape (positive real 10s30s slope of ~+45bp) and Japan's participation, since nobody has upgraded Japanese potential growth. After that conditioning LR = 1.8:1, which sits BELOW my 2:1 bar, so I use it at HALF WEIGHT: it moves my P50 by ~15bp, not ~30bp. E2, Chinese PPI +5.0% y/y and exit from deflation as a signal for the higher China path: LR ~1.4:1. BELOW 2:1, DISCOUNTED TO ZERO. E3, THE SIMULATION, treated as one piece of evidence with its own LR and explicitly NOT as a prior or as fact. Reported coupled 2032-36 growth: baseline 2.946%, central-path 2.945%, automation-boom 2.999%, ai-energy-boom 3.031%, tech-breakthrough 3.682%, net-zero 3.898%, regional-divergence 2.500%, tech-plateau 2.291%, tech-stagnation 2.299%, debt-populism 1.674%. LR = P(model prints ~2.95% | true coupled growth >= 2.6%) / P(same | < 2.6%) ~ 1.15:1, i.e. essentially uninformative, because the model's structural omissions bind identically in both worlds. I VERIFIED the three omissions the Fermi seat asserted rather than taking them on trust: src/modules/production.ts:84 sets gamma = 0.55 on useful energy (the repo itself flags this against a mainstream cost-share 0.05-0.08), and a grep across src/modules/ returns NO termPremium, NO nominal sovereign yield and NO unemployment field — the only CPI references in the repo sit in a separate energy-inflation estimand registry (src/simulations/semantic-contracts.ts), not on the macro path. So the model cannot represent Baumol, care-sector monopsony, nominal rigidity or a fiscal-dominance growth channel, which is precisely my effort's channel. DISCOUNTED to LR 1.0 for the growth level; I retain only its PPP weights. POSTERIOR: P50 1.85%, which is the Historian's 2.05 moved down 20bp — about 15bp from the half-weighted real-yield signal and the rest from the China-leg structural correction. Independent landing check: the Scout's 1.80 and Fermi's 1.70 bracket it from below and the Historian's 2.05 from above. SPREAD: I adopt the Historian's right-tail truncation argument in full (>= 3.1% requires China ~5% in 2032-36, contradicting the locked block, or OECD ~2.5%, with no 5-yr precedent since 2000-2007), so sigma_right 0.68 against sigma_left 0.78, keeping the full unconditional recession width on the left. P10 = 1.85 - 1.282(0.78) = 0.85; P90 = 1.85 + 1.282(0.68) = 2.72, set 2.75. DIRECTION: MODERATED down 20bp at the P50 and narrowed ~20bp at each tail.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:58.134Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"The ACM or Kim-Wright US 10y real term premium, as published by the New York Fed, prints BELOW +0.40% as a trailing twelve-month average, while the real 10s30s slope (DFII30 minus DFII10) flattens to under +15bp. This is the direct falsifier of the 2.5:1 likelihood ratio that carries my whole payload: it would say the 2026 duration repricing was a transient selloff of the 2013-14 type rather than a regime, and that my 0.75 -> 1.15 term-premium update was unjustified.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ4 P50 falls from 1.8% toward 1.25-1.35% (the term premium returns to the Historian's +0.75 prior or below, with r* and the wedge unchanged); CQ5 falls from 24 to roughly 15-17 as regime F loses probability; CQ6-C falls ~4pp and CQ6-B rises ~4pp. CQ3 is essentially unaffected, since my growth answer rests on the locked demographic arithmetic rather than on the yield.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An explicit G7 duration-targeting action: a stated yield or spread objective on a purchase programme, a statutory sovereign-holding requirement on banks or pension funds, or an announced issuance-maturity shortening framework, in the US, the euro area or the UK (Japan's existing wedge does not count, being already in the 2026 base).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Confirms the 42% repression branch early and moves it toward 0.75+. CQ4 P50 falls 25-35bp to ~1.45-1.55% and the P10 falls toward 0.60; CQ5 falls hard from 24 to 12-14, because a pinned curve suppresses BOTH conjuncts simultaneously; CQ2 rises 3-4pp, since the wedge is paid for in realised inflation; CQ6-C rises ~3pp at D's expense.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >= 25% during which G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yields fail to fall by 50bp, or rise by >= 25bp, inside the drawdown window. The 2022 episode is the template; the resolution test is the yield path, not the equity path.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ5's conjunct B resolves YES on its own, so CQ5 collapses to conjunct A alone and rises from 24 to roughly 36-40 (my p(A) = 0.36). It would also confirm the term-premium regime, pushing CQ4 P50 up 15-25bp, and raise AI-Q4 toward 80% by confirming the impairment channel through a correlated repricing.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's trailing three-year average headline CPI prints >= 1.6% (NBS), with core CPI holding above 1.3% and the gasoline contribution to headline below 0.15pp — i.e. the exit from deflation proves domestic rather than commodity-driven, which is exactly the discount I applied to that signal.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Retrospectively justifies the full 2.5:1 LR I cut to 1.7:1. CQ2's China conjunct falls from 0.60 to ~0.38 and CQ2 falls from 27 to roughly 17-18. It also weakens the Chinese savings-glut leg of r*, adding 10-15bp to CQ4, and raises CQ3's China leg, lifting the CQ3 P50 by ~10bp and CQ6-A by ~2pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE prints a trailing three-year average BELOW 2.0% while the US policy rate is cut below 2.5% nominal — the disinflationary-stagnation branch arriving early, most plausibly via the AI-Q4 capex impairment leaving excess datacenter and generation capacity as a deflationary power-price impulse.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my front-loaded inflation object, which is the basis of both CQ2 and the CQ6 inflation cuts. CQ2 falls from 27 to roughly 14-16; CQ6-D rises from 15 to ~24 and CQ6-C falls from 22 to ~13; CQ4 P50 falls 30-40bp as both r* and the term premium ease into a demand shortfall; hypothesis plank (c) rises from 42 toward 60, since 'falls in the 2030s' would be vindicated.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 bayes seat, effort 'rstar'."}}